.TH JSONEDIT 1 "2026-10-11" "crucible-tui" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
jsonedit \- validate and safely edit JSON, JSONC and JSON5 files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B jsonedit
[\fB\-\-dialect\fR \fBjson\fR|\fBjsonc\fR|\fBjson5\fR]
[\fB\-\-indent\fR \fBtab\fR|\fIN\fR]
.I file
.br
.B jsonedit \-\-check
[\fB\-\-dialect\fR \fBjson\fR|\fBjsonc\fR|\fBjson5\fR]
.I file
.br
.B jsonedit
\fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B jsonedit
is a visudo\-style editor for configuration files.
It copies
.I file
into a private temporary buffer, opens your editor on the buffer, and
validates the result when the editor exits.
The file is replaced only when the buffer is valid, and then with exactly
the bytes the editor left: nothing is re\-formatted or re\-serialised,
and only an explicit
.B (f)ix\ indentation
choice changes any of them.
.PP
With
.BR \-\-check ,
it validates
.I file
in place, prints any errors as
.IR FILE : LINE : COL ": message"
on standard error, and never opens an editor or writes anything.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-dialect " " \fIjson\fR | \fIjsonc\fR | \fIjson5\fR
Override the dialect chosen from the file extension.
.TP
.BR \-\-indent " " \fBtab\fR | \fIN\fR
Use tabs, or
.I N
spaces, as the reference indent style instead of detecting it.
Edit mode only; with
.B \-\-check
it is a usage error.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Validate only.
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-\-help
Print the version or a usage summary.
.SH THE PROMPT
When the edited buffer is invalid, its errors are printed and, if standard
input is a terminal,
.B jsonedit
asks:
.PP
.RS
(e)dit again, e(x)it without saving, (a)bort keeping the buffer
.RE
.PP
Answer with one letter and Enter.
.B e
reopens the same buffer;
.B x
discards the buffer and leaves the file untouched;
.B a
keeps the buffer and prints its path.
End of input counts as
.BR a .
There is no way to write an invalid buffer.
.PP
The indent unit of the original file (tabs or
.I N
spaces) is detected when one style covers at least 80% of its indented
lines; a file with fewer than three indented lines is not judged.
When the style is ambiguous and standard input is a terminal, it is asked
for once, before the editor starts.
New or changed lines that do not match it produce a warning, and the prompt
gains
.BR (w)rite\ anyway ,
because the bytes are still valid.
When at least one of those lines can be fixed, the prompt first offers
.BR (f)ix\ indentation :
it replaces only the flagged lines' leading whitespace with their bracket
depth in the file's unit (a line starting with a closing bracket dedents one
level), prints the fixed line numbers, and checks the result again without
reopening the editor.
A line that starts inside a comment or a string continuation is left alone.
Nothing is re\-indented unless you choose it.
.PP
If the file changed on disk while it was being edited, it is not
overwritten: the change is reported and the prompt offers
.B (e)dit again
or
.BR (a)bort .
.PP
When standard input is not a terminal,
.B jsonedit
never prompts: an invalid buffer or a concurrent change is reported, the
buffer is kept, its path is printed, and the exit status is 1; a style
warning is printed and the valid buffer is written.
.SH DIALECTS
A
.I .jsonc
extension selects JSONC (comments and trailing commas),
.I .json5
selects JSON5, and anything else is strict JSON.
In every dialect, duplicate keys and empty input are errors; a leading
UTF\-8 BOM is accepted and does not shift reported columns.
A JSONC file with a
.I .json
name has its comments rejected; the error names
.BR \-\-dialect .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Written, unchanged, an empty new file not created, or valid under
.BR \-\-check .
.TP
.B 1
Invalid, aborted, exited without saving, or the editor failed.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.BR VISUAL ", " EDITOR
The editor command, tried in that order, then
.BR vi .
The value is split like a shell word list (quotes and backslashes are
honoured, nothing is expanded), so
.B code \-\-wait
works.
Graphical editors must be told to wait
.RB ( "code \-\-wait" ", " "subl \-w" ),
or jsonedit sees an unchanged buffer.
A non\-zero editor exit is treated as abort.
.IP
When the editor is reopened after an invalid save or an indent warning,
known editors are asked to open at the first problem line:
.B +N FILE
for vi, vim, nvim, nano, emacs, emacsclient, micro and kak;
.B FILE:N
for hx, helix and subl;
.B \-\-goto FILE:N
for code and codium.
The program is matched by exact file name; any other editor is opened
on the buffer alone.
No hint is passed when the editor command contains
.BR \-\- ,
when a vi/vim/nvim command already uses Vim's ten startup commands,
or (for the
.B FILE:N
forms) when the buffer path contains a colon.
.SH FILES AND SAFETY
The buffer is created mode 0600 in the temporary directory
.RB ( $TMPDIR ,
else
.IR /tmp ),
and keeps the file's extension so editor syntax modes apply.
It is removed after a write or an exit without saving, and kept on abort.
.PP
Writes are atomic: a temporary file in the target's directory is written,
fsynced, given the original's permission bits (and owner, where permitted),
then renamed over the target.
A symbolic link is followed and its target replaced; the link itself is
left intact.
A missing file is created only when the buffer is valid and non\-empty.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR visudo (8),
.BR mdview (1)
